  "title": "La Grange: the Oldham County seat with trains on Main Street",
  "updated": "2026-10-19",
  "summary": "La Grange began in 1827 at a crossroads named for Lafayette’s estate, became the Oldham County seat in 1838, and still runs trains down Main Street.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "County and name",
      "items": [
        {
          "text": "Oldham County formed from Jefferson, Shelby and Henry, 74th county, named for Col. William Oldham, killed at St. Clair's Defeat 1791.",
          "sources": [
            "khs-1251"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Founding and seat fight",
      "items": [
        {
          "text": "Taylor's 1827 town at the crossroads, named for Lafayette's La Grange; court 1827, Westport sessions 1828, La Grange permanently 1838; incorporated Jan 23 1840.",
          "sources": [
            "gk",
            "khs-2594"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Railroad",
      "items": [
        {
          "text": "Louisville & Frankfort RR down Main Street; arrival 1850 or late 1850s (Grokipedia gives both). CSX 20-30 trains/day today.",
          "sources": [
            "gk"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Enslavement and education",
      "items": [
        {
          "text": "Westport court records of enslavement, Henry Bibb family (marker 2594). Rosenwald school 1921-22 (marker 2418).",
          "sources": [
            "khs-2594",
            "khs-2418"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
